1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,399
On the fifth of November, at PM Summit 2026, every breakout room in the session

2
00:00:10,419 --> 00:00:16,957
“PM Role in an AI World” will take on a real project problem with an AI agent.

3
00:00:17,357 --> 00:00:22,729
This briefing explains the situation, what to prepare, and what your room will deliver.

4
00:00:23,829 --> 00:00:27,693
Meet KESTREL: a new sensor-hub chip and its evaluation kit,

5
00:00:27,713 --> 00:00:32,521
fourteen days from tape-out, with a lead customer waiting for samples.

6
00:00:32,921 --> 00:00:36,364
Behind it stands a team across design, verification,

7
00:00:36,384 --> 00:00:39,693
test, software, quality, operations and marketing.

8
00:00:39,713 --> 00:00:44,508
Every one of them needs something from the project manager this week.

9
00:00:45,108 --> 00:00:48,832
On Monday, you inherit KESTREL as its new project manager.

10
00:00:48,852 --> 00:00:52,796
By Thursday morning, you are holding six problems at once.

11
00:00:53,196 --> 00:00:57,841
Sofia forwards a customer change request that nobody can answer yet.

12
00:00:58,091 --> 00:01:03,241
Chen mentions a supplier delay, in a risk log no one has touched in five weeks.

13
00:01:03,491 --> 00:01:09,080
Priya flags a late load board, in a meeting where nobody writes down what was decided.

14
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:11,203
Marco’s numbers say one thing.

15
00:01:11,223 --> 00:01:14,137
Last week’s status report still says green.

16
00:01:14,387 --> 00:01:19,266
Aiko’s design is not ready, and steering needs a decision by two o’clock.

17
00:01:19,516 --> 00:01:23,382
And the lessons Jonas left behind arrive three days too late.

18
00:01:23,982 --> 00:01:25,552
None of this is unusual.

19
00:01:25,572 --> 00:01:28,554
It is the week most project managers know.

20
00:01:28,804 --> 00:01:32,180
And none of it is the part of the job that matters most:

21
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,068
decisions, stakeholders, and thinking ahead.

22
00:01:36,268 --> 00:01:41,196
Before the summit, we ask for about twenty minutes of preparation, in four steps.

23
00:01:41,546 --> 00:01:41,778
One.

24
00:01:41,798 --> 00:01:46,066
Read “Fourteen Days”, the KESTREL story attached to your invitation.

25
00:01:46,086 --> 00:01:48,177
It takes about twelve minutes.

26
00:01:48,477 --> 00:01:48,732
Two.

27
00:01:48,752 --> 00:01:52,304
Open your room’s case file at the back of the story.

28
00:01:52,324 --> 00:01:55,807
Your invitation tells you which one, from C1 to C6.

29
00:01:55,827 --> 00:02:01,729
Read it as the project manager, and ask: which parts of this work would I hand off?

30
00:02:02,029 --> 00:02:02,368
Three.

31
00:02:02,388 --> 00:02:03,446
Map your own week.

32
00:02:03,466 --> 00:02:06,262
How long does this task take you, step by step?

33
00:02:06,282 --> 00:02:10,195
Mark which steps are repetitive, and which need your judgment.

34
00:02:10,495 --> 00:02:10,772
Four.

35
00:02:10,792 --> 00:02:16,474
Bring one idea for what you would do with those hours back: a decision you would prepare better,

36
00:02:16,494 --> 00:02:20,198
a stakeholder you would see sooner, or time to think ahead.

37
00:02:20,698 --> 00:02:22,863
You don’t need any AI experience.

38
00:02:22,883 --> 00:02:27,585
Rooms mix beginners and experts on purpose, and everyone has a role.

39
00:02:27,735 --> 00:02:30,226
Please don’t bring confidential documents.

40
00:02:30,246 --> 00:02:32,837
Everything you need is in the case file.

41
00:02:34,037 --> 00:02:37,091
On the day, each room takes one of the six jobs.

42
00:02:37,191 --> 00:02:39,888
In sixty minutes, you map where the hours go,

43
00:02:39,908 --> 00:02:45,120
design an agent that takes the repetitive part, and measure the time it gives back.

44
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:52,045
Your room leaves with a Pilot Pack: the agent’s instructions, the decisions the project manager keeps,

45
00:02:52,066 --> 00:02:58,861
the hours it gives back, one guardrail, an owner, and a first test on a real project after the summit.

46
00:02:59,961 --> 00:03:01,634
The agent takes the routine.

47
00:03:01,654 --> 00:03:04,999
You take back the time for the work only you can do.

48
00:03:05,699 --> 00:03:08,370
Read “Fourteen Days” before the fifth of November.

49
00:03:08,390 --> 00:03:09,990
We’ll see you in the room.
